Andy Johnson <usenet@REMOVETHIS.swervy.tk> wrote in message news:<cmvanp$hrh$1@joe.rice.edu>...
> Does anyone else have a lettering scheme for their inventory items? I 
> always put certain items in certain letter slots, because my fingers 
> have gotten used to using those key combinations. I've assumed that 
> everyone else does the same thing, but now I'm actually wondering if 
> this is the case.

Yes.  Some of them are natural (k for key/creditcard/lockpick, w for
magic whistle) but a lot of them are based on other things, often what
letter they were on thte first time I ever found one (or at least the
first time I really used it).
a - primary weapon (since it starts here)
b - secondary weapon
q - stack of daggers (for "quiver").
e - pick axe.  I played a lot of archaeologists, and it often started
here.  It's reflex now to hit ae(direction) to dig, though autodig has
superceded that.
l - unicorn horn.  First time I figured out you could apply it, it was
here.
o - bag of holding.  First one I found was here.
x - lamp.  No idea why.
z - whatever wand I'm trying to wrest the last charge out of

I've never had special slots for armor but I probably will now--I like
the idea of (T)shirt, (B)oots, (A)rmor, (G)loves, (R)obe, (S)hield,
(H)elmet, and I never use the uppercase letters for things I often
change.  I'd probably put water-walking boots at W in that scheme if I
have them and speed boots.  And that'd conveniently leave slow
(D)igestion, (F)ree action, and (L)evitation too.

